<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Princess Wencheng</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/680">680</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Princess Wencheng was a member of the Tang imperial clan who was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> in 641 as a bride for the royal house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a>, sealing peace with the emperor Taizong after a Tibetan attack on the Tang frontier. Tibetan tradition credits her with bringing the Jowo statue of the Buddha now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokhang">Jokhang</a>, and she became a lasting symbol of Sino-Tibetan relations.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/princess-wencheng.jpg" alt="Princess Wencheng" width="276" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ernst Stavro Blofeld · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Princess_Wencheng.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Princess Wencheng belonged to a minor branch of the imperial clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>. Both Chinese and Tibetan sources agree that she was not a daughter of the Chinese emperor. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> she is also called Gyasa, meaning Chinese wife. In 634 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a> sent an envoy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, and on hearing that the Turks and the Tuyuhun had marriage alliances with the Tang he proposed a marriage. The Tang refused, and the envoy told Songtsen that they had first accepted but that the Tuyuhun had changed their decision. Songtsen attacked the Tuyuhun and the Dangxiang.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Songtsen stationed large numbers of troops on the western border of Songzhou, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, and demanded a princess in marriage or else he would lead 50,000 troops against it. In 638 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> attacked Songzhou and took two prefectures, but withdrew after Tang counterattacks. Tibetan sources claim victory, and Chinese sources claim it for the Tang. In 640 Songtsen made a new proposal, which the Tang accepted to prevent further attacks. In 641 Wencheng left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gar-tongtsen">Gar Tongtsen Yulsung</a> and married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a>.</p><p>According to the Old Book of Tang, Songtsen received her at Bohai Lake and built a palace for her. She disliked the Tibetan custom of painting faces red, and he banned it. The Tang monk Xuanzhao, passing through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, was warmly received by her. Tibetan histories say she found Tibet shaped like an indigenous demoness, Srinmo, lying on her back, and that temples were built to suppress her. She oversaw the building of the Ramoche Temple and, with Songtsen's wife from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, helped him with national affairs and the growth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Tibetan sources call her Rgya-Mo-Bzav, the goddess from the Central Empire, and hold her to be a manifestation of White Tara, as Songtsen's first wife, the Nepali princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhrikuti">Bhrikuti</a>, is of Green Tara. Tradition says the seated statue of Sakyamuni she brought is enshrined in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokhang">Jokhang</a>. A thangka she embroidered is kept at Tradruk Temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nedong">Nêdong</a>. Chinese classical literature treated her as an insignificant figure, while from about 1928 Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> nationalist writing made her a transmitter of technology. Since the 2000s the Chinese state has presented an opera about her in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>, which many observers say misrepresents Tibetan history.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Princess Wencheng” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 634 he sent his first embassy to Tang China, and after a Tibetan attack on the Tang frontier at Songzhou in 638 the emperor Taizong agreed to a marriage alliance: <b>Princess Wencheng</b> arrived in Tibet in 641.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=29.65,91.1&amp;year=670" aria-label="Tibet on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6921 1080 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Princess Wencheng"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7371 1350h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7371 1350h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=29.65,91.1&amp;year=670">What was here in 670 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
